-g2# or Qf1#? Not immediate but winning.  \n\nSo Qe2+ seems strong, but Bf3+ first might be even more forcing leading to immediate mate if king goes to g1. Let’s check Bf3+ first: after Bf3+, if Kg1 (only legal), then Qe2# — yes, mate.  \nBut what if White has a defensive resource? Is there any piece that can block Bf3+? No, because bishop moves from g4 to f3, checking king on f1. No piece between. So Bf3+ forces Kg1. Then Qe2# — mate.  \n\nThus **B: g4-f3+** followed by Qe2# is forced mate in two.  \nWe must check if any of White’s pieces can interpose on f2 or capture the bishop on f3 after Bf3+.  \n- f2 square: empty (no pawn).  \n- Capturing bishop on f3: White has no piece that can capture on f3 (queen missing, knight missing, pawns not in range).  \nSo indeed mate in two.  \n\nTherefore, best move is **B: g4-f3** (check).  \n\nReason: This move delivers immediate check and forces the king to g1, setting up discovered checkmate with queen to e2 next move. White has no defense. It ends the game quickest.  \n\nAnswer: B: g4-f3'}
