, f2, f3, g1, g3, h1, h2, h3. After Kh1, we could play Qf1# if queen can reach f1 next turn. But after Qg2+, Kh1, our next move we could play Qf1# – but does our queen move from g2 to f1? Yes, it's a queen move horizontally one square, legal. So Qg2+ leads to mate next move.  \n  3. **Q: e4-h4** – not check but threatens Qh2# next? White could play Kh1 or Kg1 maybe. After Qh4, white’s only moves are Kh1, Kg1, Kg2. But Qh4 controls h2 and g5... but not immediate mate.  \n\nLooking more carefully: After Qg2+, Kh1. Next turn we can play Qf1# (from g2 to f1) unless white has something else (but they only have king). However, we must also check our own king safety: after Qg2+, if white had any piece that could give check, but they don't. So Qg2+ forces Kh1, then Qf1#.  \nBut wait: Is Qg2+ a check? Queen on e4 to g2: e4 to g2 is diagonal (up-left? Actually from Black perspective, e4 to g2 is down-right? Coordinates: e4 (file e rank 4) to g2 (file g rank 2) – that's two files right, two ranks down. Diagonal movement, yes. White king is on h2, so g2 is adjacent to h2 (one square left of king). So queen on g2 attacks h2 (king’s square) because queen on g2 controls h2 (same rank? Actually g2 and h2 are adjacent horizontally, queen controls horizontally). So Qg2+ indeed checks king. King’s legal moves from h2: h1, g1, g2 (but g2 is attacked by queen), g3, h3. But g2 is occupied by queen, so not allowed. Also g1? Not attacked by queen? Queen on g2 attacks g1 (vertical down one square). Actually queen on g2 attacks g1 (same file, down one), attacks g3 (up one), attacks h2 (right one), attacks h1 (diagonal down-right? Wait coordinates: g2 to h1 is diagonal one square? Yes, file g to h is right, rank 2 to 1 is down, diagonal). So queen on g2 attacks h1, g1, h2, h3, g3, and all squares along those lines. So from h2, king cannot go to h1 (attacked), g1 (attacked), g2 (occupied), g3 (attacked), h3 (attacked). Actually queen attacks h3? From g2 to h3 is diagonal up-right? g to h is right, 2 to 3 is up, so yes diagonal. So every square around h2 is attacked or occupied. So Qg2# is mate immediately? Let's verify: King on h2, queen on g2 attacks all squares around h2 (h1, g1, g2 occupied, g3, h3, also f2, f1 etc). But specifically: squares adjacent to h2: g1, g2, g3, h1, h3. All attacked. So Qg2 is checkmate! Because king has no legal moves and is in check.  \nYes! So Qg2 is checkmate.\n\nReason: Qe4-g2 delivers immediate checkmate. No other move does that in one move. Some queen moves threaten mate next move, but immediate mate is best.\n\nAnswer: Q: e4-g2"}
