\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |KWF1| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a4-a3, P: b4-b3, K: d5-c4, K: d5-c5, K: d5-c6, K: d5-d4, K: d5-d6, K: d5-e4, K: d5-e5, K: d5-e6, P: f7-f5, P: f7-f6, P: g7-g5, P: g7-g6]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: This is an endgame scenario with only kings and pawns. Black has a king at d5 and pawns at a4, b4, f7, g7. White has a king at h1 and no other pieces visible. Black's pawns are advanced, especially on the kingside (f7, g7), while the queenside pawns (a4, b4) are already on the 4th rank. White's king is far away in the corner. Black is clearly ahead and should push pawns to promote and checkmate.\n\nEvaluation:  \n1. Pawn moves like f7-f5 or f7-f6 advance the f-pawn, but f6 might block the g-pawn.  \n2. g7-g5 or g7-g6 similarly advance the g-pawn.  \n3. King moves like d5-c4 or d5-e4 bring the king closer to support pawn advancement.  \n4. a4-a3 or b4-b3 advance queenside pawns but are less urgent because they are already far ahead and the white king is far away.\n\nReason: The key is to create a passed pawn and promote quickly. The f- and g-pawns are connected and can support each other. Moving g7-g5 is solid, but g7-g6 might be slightly passive. f7-f5 opens up the f-file and prepares f5-f4-f3 etc., but f7-f6 is more conservative. The king should centralize to support the pawns. Given the white king's distance, pushing the pawns aggressively is best. I'll choose g7-g5 to gain space on the kingside and potentially open lines for the king later. This move also keeps flexibility for f7-f6 or f7-f5 next.\n\nAnswer: P: g7-g5"}
