 with f7.  \n\n**Reason**:  \nThe most forcing and safest winning plan is to eliminate White’s only real threat – the f7 pawn – with the King. **K: e5-d6** simultaneously:  \n- Attacks f7 (if White plays f7-f6 next, Kd6 prevents f6-f5-f4-f3 promotion path because King controls f6).  \n- Prepares Kd6-e7 next, trapping f7.  \n- Cuts off White King from coming to e6/e5 to help.  \n- Leaves queenside pawns safe (a4/b4 can be pushed later after White’s pawn is gone).  \n\nThis leaves Black with an extra pawn and winning king-and-pawn endgame. Other moves allow White counterplay (like f7-f5 followed by Kg3/Kf4 approaching).  \n\n**Answer**: K: e5-d6'}
