rom f3, if attacked by pawn at g4, it must move to e2, e3, f2, f4? But f4 is attacked by g5 pawn? Actually after g5-g4, the pawn is now on g4, so f4 is not attacked by that pawn? g4 pawn attacks f3 and h3? Actually pawn attacks diagonally forward: Black pawn on g4 attacks f3 and h3 (since Black moves downward, ranks decrease). So f3 is attacked, and h3 is empty. So White king cannot stay on f3. Available squares: e2, e3, f2, f4? f4 is not attacked by g4 pawn? g4 pawn attacks f3 and h3, not f4. But f4 might be attacked by Black king? Currently not. So f4 is a possible escape. But after g4, White king could go to f4, attacking our g4 pawn? Yes, Kf3-f4 attacks g4 pawn. But then our king can defend g4 from f5 or e5? Our king at d6 can move to e5 or f5 to defend. But if we play g5-g4, we need to see if we can maintain the pawn. Alternatively, we can play g5-g4 and then if Kf4, we can play Kd6-e6 defending g4? Actually Ke6 does not defend g4: e6 to g4 is two files right and two ranks down, not adjacent. So Ke6 does not defend g4. We would need Kf5 or Ke5. But after g4, if Kf4, we might play Kd6-e5? That would defend g4 and attack f4, forking king and pawn? Actually Ke5 attacks f4 square, so White king would have to move from f4, and then we can push pawns. That seems good.\n\nOther candidate: f7-f5? Not as forcing. \n\ng5-g4 seems the most aggressive, putting White king in immediate danger and gaining space. \n\nAlso note: White has no pawns, so our only concern is to promote a pawn while avoiding stalemate. With 4 pawns, we should win easily if we don't blunder. But we must be careful not to allow White king to capture pawns. g5-g4 might allow White king to capture if undefended, but our king is close enough to defend.\n\nCheck concrete lines:\n- g5-g4, Kf3-f4 (attacking g4). Then Black: Kd6-e5! Now White king attacked, must move from f4. Options: Ke2, e3, f3, f5, g3, g5? Actually from f4, squares attacked by Black king at e5: f4, f5, f6, e4, e6, d4, d5, d6? Actually e5 