n, so pushing g3 unsupported might lose g-pawn. Safer: Kd6–c5 first.  \n\n**Reason for final choice:**  \nKing move Kd6–c5 is active, centralizes toward queenside pawns, cuts off White king’s path to b4/a4 via d4, and prepares to support pawn advances next turn while staying safe from checks. It improves Black’s winning chances in this king+pawns endgame by coordinating king with the passed pawns.\n\n**Answer:**  \nSituation: Endgame with Black up 4 pawns vs White’s bare king, Black pawns advanced on queenside.  \nEvaluation: Kd6–c5 centralizes king, blocks White king, supports pawn promotion.  \nReason: Best for king activity and pawn protection; other pawn moves risk pawn loss without compensation.  \nAnswer: K: d6-c5'}
