\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a4-a3, P: b4-b3, K: c5-b5, K: c5-b6, K: c5-c4, K: c5-c6, K: c5-d4, K: c5-d5, K: c5-d6, P: f7-f5, P: f7-f6, P: g4-g3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': '**Situation:**  \nBoard is sparse — only a few pawns and kings remain. White has a king at f4 (KWF1) and pawns at a4, b4, g4 (all PBF1). Black has king at c5 (KBF1) and a pawn at f7 (PBF0) that hasn’t moved. The other black pawn at f? Wait — the board shows "f7" is PBF0. Actually checking: black pawn at f7, white pawns at a4, b4, g4, plus black’s own pawns at a4, b4, g4? No — those pawns at a4/b4/g4 are labeled PBF1, meaning they’re *Black* pawns, not White. Wait, piece codes:  \nPBF1 = Black pawn, moved already. KWF1 = White king moved already.  \nSo Black pawns on a4, b4, g4 (all PBF1), plus black pawn f7 (PBF0).  \nWhite has only a king on f4 (KWF1). No white pawns visible.  \n\nThis means White has no pawns — just a lone king. Black has 4 pawns. Black to move.  \n\n**Evaluation:**  \nSince White has no pawns, a pawn promotion will be decisive if we can safely push pawns. But White king is near g4 and f4; our black king is at c5.  \n\nKey candidate moves:  \n1. **P: g4-g3** — pushes passed pawn closer to promotion. King at f4 is nearby, but after g3, White king could capture g3 (f4xg3) unless our king defends. Our king is far though. If Kxg3, we lose the pawn. Could be risky but then we still have other pawns.  \n2. **P: f7-f5** — advances central pawn two squares (first move). This blocks f4 king’s access to g5? Actually puts pawn on f5, attacked by king f4. King could take f5 next move? If Kxf5, Kxf5?? Our king can’t defend f5 from c5. So f5 may be immediately lost.  \n3. **P: f7-f6** — safer advance, f6 guarded by king c5? No, not immediately. But f6 is not attacked by f4 king (king controls f4,g4,g5 etc). Actually f4 king attacks f5, g5, e5, f3, g3, e3, e4, g4. f6 is not in its move range. So f7-f6 is safe from king capture now.  \n4. **King moves** — might centralize king toward pawns to defend them for promotion.\n\nBut the biggest threat to winning is not messing up pawns before we can escort them to promotion. Since White can only chase pawns with king, we should advance pawns where king can’t immediately capture, and use our king to block White king.\n\nCurrently White king at f4 attacks g4 pawn. Actually f4 king attacks g4? Yes, f4 to g4 is one square, so g4 pawn is attacked now! In fact, White can capture g4 next turn! That’s critical — our g4 pawn is hanging.  \n\nOur valid moves:  \n- P: g4-g3 gets it out of attack? Yes, g3 is still attacked by f4 (f4xg3 possible). Actually f4 to g3 is one square diagonally? No, f4 to g3 is forward one and left one? Wait coordinates: f4 (file f, rank 4) to g3 (file g, rank 3) is diagonal: f→g is +1 file, rank 4→3 is -1 rank, yes diagonal. So White king at f4 can capture on g3. So g4-g3 doesn’t save pawn from king.  \n\nThus we must defend g4 or move it where White king can’t take, or move king to defend g4. Our king at c5 can move to d4 (K: c5-d4) to defend g4? No, d4 to g4 is too far. Actually d4 is 3 squares away from g4. To defend g4 we need king on f5 or e4 etc. None possible in one move.  \n\nWe could move pawn a4-a3 or b4-b3 — they are safe from White king (far away).  \n\nBut losing g4 pawn is avoidable:  \nOption A: Move king toward e5/d5/e4 to defend g4 later.  \nOption B: Push f7-f6 to create counter-threat? Doesn’t defend g4.  \nOption C: Sacrifice g4 but promote others faster? But we can save it.  \n\nLet’s check:  \nK: c5-d4 — king moves to d4. Then White king could take g4: Kf4xg4. Our king d4 is 3 files away from g4, can’t recapture. Then White has a pawn (just converted a pawn into his advantage? no, no pawns). Actually we’d just lose a pawn for nothing.  \n\nBut after Kf4xg4, our king d4 could go e5 or e4 attacking f5, g5 etc. But that’s worse material.  \n\nBetter: move K: c5-d5. That attacks f4? Actually d5 to f4 is two squares diagonally, not in king move range. But d5 defends e6, f5? No. But it blocks White king from e5, and next turn we can Kd5-e5 defending f5 if we push f7-f5, but maybe better immediate defense: from d5, g4 is still 3 squares away.  \n\nActually only move that defends g4 directly this turn is none. So g4 is lost unless we move it where it’s safe. Where is safe from f4 king? Squares not attacked by f4: h4, g2, etc. We can’t get there in one pawn move.  \n\nThus maybe let White take g4 while we push f7-f6 or a4-a3, b4-b3 toward promotion.  \n\nLet’s see White king moves after taking g4: Kf4xg4. Then our pawns: a4, b4, f7. Our king c5. Our plan: king escorts pawns.  \n\nBetter move: **P: f7-f6** — advances pawn safely, no capture threat. White king can move to g5 next? f4→g5 possible (up one). g5 attacks f6. So if we play f7-f6, then White Kf4-g5 next attacks f6. Our king can defend f6 from e6 or d6 etc.  \n\nBut if White Kf4xg4 immediately instead, then after f7-f6, g4 king is far from our a/b pawns, we queen fastest.  \n\nSo maybe best: push a or b pawns first, ignore g4 loss.  \n\nCheck: P: a4-a3 — now a3 pawn, defended by king? No.  \nP: b4-b3 — b3 pawn, White king can’t reach quickly.  \n\nWhite king is near g4–f4–e4–f5–g5 area. Distant from queenside.  \n\nSo: Push a or b pawn. Which? b3 gets closer but king far; but safe.  \n\nAlso consider **K: c5-b5** — centralizes toward queenside to support a/b pawns while leaving g4 hanging.  \n\nBut the immediate win condition is to promote a pawn before White can catch it with king. Our king can help on queenside.  \n\nLet’s simulate: 1. P: b4-b3. White: Kf4xg4. 2. P: a4-a3. White: Kg4-f5 heading to e6? Actually targets f6? We didn’t push f6 yet. We can push f7-f6 later.  \n\nBut b3 pawn is 3 moves from queening (b3–b2–b1Q). White king from g4 to b1 takes about 6 moves if unobstructed. Our king can block.  \n\nSo safest winning plan: Push queenside pawns, let g4 be captured, use king to protect queening path.  \n\nThus first move: **P: b4-b3** is best — advances queenside passer, keeps a pawn.  \n\nAlternate: K: c5-b5 (supports a4, b4 pawns).  \n\nBut b4-b3 immediately gains a tempo.  \n\n**Final choice: P: b4-b3**  \n\n**Reason:**  \n- White king is stuck on kingside and can capture g4 next, but g4 is irrelevant to queening race.  \n- b3 starts pawn’s journey to promotion, and with our king coming to c4 or b4 soon, we can protect it from white king’s approach.  \n- Other pawns on a4 and f7 remain as extra promotion chances. This maximizes winning chances by starting queening process immediately and avoiding risky central pawn moves that could be attacked quickly.  \n\n---\n\n**Answer: P: b4-b3**'}
