\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a4-a3, P: b3-b2, K: c5-b4, K: c5-b5, K: c5-b6, K: c5-c4, K: c5-c6, K: c5-d4, K: c5-d5, K: c5-d6, P: f7-f5, P: f7-f6]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Situation:  \nWe are playing as Black. The board is nearly empty with only a few pawns and kings. Material count: Black has king (c5), pawns on a4, b3, f7. White has king (g4) and one pawn on f7 is actually Black\'s f7 pawn (PBF0) — so it seems White has no pawns. Black has three pawns, White has none. This means Black is up three pawns, which is a huge material advantage in an endgame. White king is on g4, Black king on c5. Black pawns are on a4, b3, f7. The goal is to promote one of these pawns while keeping the king safe and blocking White king from capturing them.  \n\nEvaluation:  \nPossible moves:  \n- Pawn moves: a4→a3 moves pawn backward? Wait, Black pawns move up the board from rank 7 to 1? No — in standard chess, Black pawns move downward (toward rank 1). So here coordinates: Black starts top ranks (7–8), so pawns move down the board toward rank 1. Then a4→a3 is moving *forward* (down) toward promotion. Similarly b3→b2 is moving forward (toward rank 1). f7→f6 or f5 is moving forward (down) from rank 7.  \n- King moves: Many king moves; king can help advance pawns or block White king.  \n\nWhite king is on g4, which is far from our queenside pawns (a4,b3) but closer to f7 pawn. f7 pawn is backward — not yet moved (PBF0 means never moved). If we move f7→f6 or f7→f5, White king can come toward it (f5 or e5), potentially attacking it. But our king can defend.  \n\nMost promising:  \n1. **P: f7-f5** — Opens pawn toward promotion, but moves two squares? Wait, PBF0 means never moved, so f7→f5 is legal as double-step? Actually f7→f5 is a double-step (from rank 7 to rank 5). Is that allowed? In chess, pawn on starting rank (rank 7 for Black) can move two squares forward (to rank 5). The valid moves list includes "P: f7-f5", so yes. This speeds pawn advance, but makes it vulnerable to en passant? White has no pawns on e5 or g5, so no en passant possible. But White king might attack it from g4 after f5? Actually f5 is 3 squares away from g4 (g4 to f5 is distance 1 vertically? Wait: g4 and f5 are diagonal adjacent? g4 to f5: file g→f (adjacent), rank 4→5 (down for White? White king perspective: from g4, f5 is one step diagonally forward in Black’s direction? Actually from White’s view, Black’s move direction is down board toward rank 1, so g4 to f5 is down-right for White’s king movement, which is possible in one move. So after f7-f5, White king can move to f5 capturing pawn next move unless defended. Our king is far (c5 to f5 is 3 squares away), can’t defend immediately. So f7-f5 may lose the pawn.  \n\n2. **P: f7-f6** — One step forward, safe from king capture (f6 is two squares from g4? Actually g4 to f6: file g→f (adjacent), rank 4→6 (two steps) — king moves one square, so not reachable in one move). So f7-f6 is safer. But slower.  \n\n3. Pawn moves on queenside: a4-a3 advances pawn, b3-b2 advances pawn to 7th rank (b2) (Black’s 7th rank is b2? Wait: Black starts at rank 7–8, so promotion happens at rank 1, so b3→b2 is one step from promotion at b1. Very close to promoting.  \n\nWhite king is on g4, far from b2 (distance: file g→b = 5 files, rank 4→2 = 2 ranks, total king moves ~5 moves). Our king can go to b4 or c4 to protect b3/b2 pawn. b3-b2 now means pawn is on b2, one step from queening. White king cannot catch if we defend.  \n\nAlso, our king can move toward queenside to support pawns: e.g., K: c5-b4 covers a4 and b3 pawns from being attacked (White king can’t attack them if our king is near).  \n\nOne candidate: **K: c5-b4** — gets closer to queenside pawns, protects b3 and a4. Then next turn b3-b2, then b2-b1=Q.  \n\nAlternatively **b3-b2** immediately, then White king might try h4–g3–f2–e2–d2–c2–b2? That’s many moves, our king can intercept.  \n\nBut b3-b2 now leaves b3 empty, our king not yet defending b2 pawn, so White king might try to rush toward b2. Let’s compute fastest White king route: g4 → f3 → e2 → d2 → c2 → b2 (5 moves). Our king from c5 can reach b2 in: c5 → c4 → c3 → b2 (3 moves) if needed. So we can queen before White can capture.  \n\nThus, b3-b2 seems strong. Also a4-a3 doesn’t threaten immediate promotion, so less urgent.  \n\nBut f7-f6 is safe, keeps pawn, but kingside pawn is slower to queen than b-pawn.  \n\nGiven material advantage, the quickest path to promotion is key. b3-b2 leaves pawn one step from queening, forcing White king to chase, while our king can protect or block.  \n\nAlso, check if White king can attack our king? White king on g4, Black king on c5 — no check.  \n\nReason:  \nBest is **P: b3-b2** — it advances the pawn closest to promotion, creating an immediate queening threat. White king is too far to stop it if we defend with our king next move. This maximizes winning chances because converting a pawn to a queen wins easily.  \n\nAnswer: P: b3-b2'}
