\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a3-a2, K: b3-a2, K: b3-a4, K: b3-b2, K: b3-b4, K: b3-c2, K: b3-c3, K: b3-c4]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: The board is nearly empty with only three pieces: a black pawn at a3 (PBF1), a black knight at b3 (KBF1), and a white knight at h3 (KWF1). All pieces have moved before (Moved flag 1). White likely just moved their knight to h3.  \n\nEvaluation: The position is simplified; both sides have knights, but Black has an extra pawn at a3. That pawn is one square away from promotion (a3→a2→a1). However, the white knight on h3 is far away and cannot immediately stop the pawn's advance if Black pushes it.  \n\nPotential moves:  \n- **P: a3-a2**: Advances the pawn to a2, one step from promotion. White's knight is far and would need several moves to capture it (e.g., h3→g1→f3→d2→c1→a2). This seems promising.  \n- **K: b3-a4**: Moves knight away from the pawn but doesn't create immediate threats.  \n- **K: b3-c4**: Centralizes the knight but leaves pawn unprotected.  \n- **K: b3-b4**: Moves knight forward but not directly useful.  \n\nReason: The pawn on a3 is Black's only material advantage. Advancing it immediately forces White to respond and may tie the white knight to stopping promotion. Since the knight on h3 is distant, pushing the pawn now creates winning chances by promoting to a queen. Therefore, the best move is to advance the pawn.  \n\nAnswer: P: a3-a2"}
